# JavaScript Objects

**JavaScript objects** are a way of putting related data and functionality in one place. They are arguably the most important building block in JavaScript, and once you start looking you will find them everywhere.

An object is a collection of **key-value pairs**. The keys are strings, and the values can be any JavaScript type. The idea is that you look up a value by its key. Think of a dictionary, paper or electronic: you look up a word and get its definition. In that analogy the word is the key and the definition is the value.

## Creating an Object

We create an object with curly braces, and inside them we place key-value pairs:

```{javascript autorun}
const person = {
  name: 'Grace',
  age: 30
};

console.log(person);
```

Each key-value pair is called a **property**. In `name: 'Grace'`, the key is `name` and the value is `'Grace'`; another way to say it is that we are setting `name` to be `'Grace'`. Pairs are separated by commas, and there can be as many as you like. An object can be written on one line or split over as many lines as you want; the two forms mean the same thing.

## Reading a Property

To get the value of a key, write the object, then a period, then the key. This is called **dot notation**.

```{javascript autorun}
const person = {
  name: 'Grace',
  age: 30
};

console.log(person.name);
console.log(person.age);
```

If you ask for a key that was never set, you get `undefined` back, in the same way an array gives `undefined` for an index that is not there:

```{javascript autorun}
const person = { name: 'Grace' };

console.log(person.nickname);
```

## Adding, Changing, and Removing Properties

The same dot syntax adds a new property. Assigning to a key that does not exist creates it:

```{javascript autorun}
const person = { name: 'Grace', age: 30 };

person.city = 'Troy';

console.log(person);
```

That has exactly the same effect as having written `city: 'Troy'` inside the original braces.

Assigning to a key that already exists changes its value instead:

```{javascript autorun}
const person = { name: 'Ada', age: 36 };

person.age = 37;
console.log(person.age);

person.age = person.age + 1;
console.log(person.age);

person.age++;
console.log(person.age);
```

The last two lines do the same thing. `person.age++` is the shorthand, the same `++` we used to step a loop variable along.

Notice that all of this works even though `person` was declared with `const`. `const` prevents the *variable* from being pointed at a different object; it does not stop us changing what is inside the object.

To remove a property entirely, use the **`delete`** statement:

```{javascript autorun}
const person = { name: 'Grace', age: 30 };

delete person.name;

console.log(person);
console.log(person.name);
```

## Methods

A property's value can be any type, including a function. When a function is stored as a property, we call it a **method**. In other words, a method is a function that belongs to an object.

```{javascript autorun}
const person = {
  name: 'Grace',
  age: 30,
  greet: function () {
    console.log('hi');
  }
};

person.greet();
```

We call a method the same way we call any function: with parentheses after it. `person.greet` is the expression that produces the function, and the `()` calls it. Leave the parentheses off and you get the function itself back, exactly as we saw in the Invoking Functions lesson.

You have been calling methods all along without knowing it. `console.log` is a function stored on an object called `console`, which makes it a method.

## Nested Objects

A value can also be another object. These are **nested objects**:

```{javascript autorun}
const person = {
  name: 'Ada',
  age: 36,
  address: {
    street: '123 Maple',
    city: 'Ann Arbor',
    state: 'MI'
  }
};

console.log(person.address);
console.log(person.address.city);
console.log(person.address.state);
```

Read `person.address.state` from left to right. `person` is the outer object, `person.address` is the object stored under the key `address`, and `person.address.state` is the value stored under `state` inside that. The dots chain as deep as the nesting goes.

When you log a whole object in a real browser console, nested objects appear collapsed with a small triangle beside them; clicking it expands the object so you can see what is inside.

## Bracket Notation

There is a second way to read a property: put the key as a string inside square brackets.

```{javascript autorun}
const person = { name: 'Grace', age: 30 };

console.log(person['name']);
console.log(person['age']);
```

Dot notation is what you will write most of the time. Bracket notation earns its place when the key is not a valid name for a variable, such as a key with a space in it, or when the key you want is sitting in a variable rather than being known in advance.

## Objects Inside Arrays

Objects and arrays combine well, and an array of objects is one of the most common shapes of data you will meet. Suppose we are running a shop and tracking inventory. The inventory is an array, and each item is an object:

```{javascript autorun}
const inventory = [
  { name: 'Pen', quantity: 10 },
  { name: 'Notebook', quantity: 5 },
  { name: 'Pencil', quantity: 0 }
];

for (const product of inventory) {
  if (product.quantity > 0) {
    console.log(product.name);
  }
}
```

The `for...of` loop hands us one object at a time as `product`, and inside the loop we read `product.name` and `product.quantity` the way we would on any object. The `if` keeps out anything with a quantity of zero, so `Pencil` is skipped and we see the items that are actually in stock.

We built all of these objects by hand. There are also a great many objects built into JavaScript and into the browser, and those are the subject of the next lesson.

## Exercises

1. Create an object named `book` with a `title` property set to `JavaScript Basics` and a `pages` property set to `120`. Then read the title into `bookTitle` using dot notation.

    ```{javascript}
    const book = {
      // Add your properties here
    };

    const bookTitle = /* Your code here */;

    console.log(bookTitle);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const book = {
      title: 'JavaScript Basics',
      pages: 120
    };

    const bookTitle = book.title;

    console.log(bookTitle);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(book.title).should('equal', 'JavaScript Basics').run('Give `book` a `title` property set to `JavaScript Basics`', 'Set the title property');
    wrap(book.pages).should('equal', 120).run('Give `book` a `pages` property set to `120`', 'Set the pages property');
    wrap(bookTitle).should('equal', 'JavaScript Basics').run('Set `bookTitle` to `book.title`', 'Read the title with dot notation');
    ```

2. Starting from the `city` object below, add a `state` property set to `Michigan`, change `population` to `123851`, and delete the `nickname` property.

    ```{javascript}
    const city = {
      name: 'Ann Arbor',
      population: 121890,
      nickname: 'Tree Town'
    };

    // Write your code here

    console.log(city);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const city = {
      name: 'Ann Arbor',
      population: 121890,
      nickname: 'Tree Town'
    };

    city.state = 'Michigan';
    city.population = 123851;
    delete city.nickname;

    console.log(city);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(city.state).should('equal', 'Michigan').run('Add a `state` property set to `Michigan`', 'Added the state property');
    wrap(city.population).should('equal', 123851).run('Change `population` to `123851`', 'Updated the population');
    wrap(typeof city.nickname).should('equal', 'undefined').run('Remove the `nickname` property with `delete`', 'Deleted the nickname property');
    ```

3. Read the `state` out of the nested `address` object and store it in `personState`.

    ```{javascript}
    const person = {
      name: 'Ada',
      address: {
        street: '123 Maple',
        city: 'Ann Arbor',
        state: 'MI'
      }
    };

    const personState = /* Your code here */;

    console.log(personState);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const person = {
      name: 'Ada',
      address: {
        street: '123 Maple',
        city: 'Ann Arbor',
        state: 'MI'
      }
    };

    const personState = person.address.state;

    console.log(personState);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(personState).should('equal', 'MI').run('Chain the dots: `person.address.state`', 'Read the nested value');
    ```

4. Loop over `inventory` with a `for...of` loop and log the name of every item whose `quantity` is `0`, so that the output is `Pencil` and then `Eraser`.

    ```{javascript}
    const inventory = [
      { name: 'Pen', quantity: 10 },
      { name: 'Pencil', quantity: 0 },
      { name: 'Notebook', quantity: 5 },
      { name: 'Eraser', quantity: 0 }
    ];

    // Write your loop here
    ```
    ```+solution
    const inventory = [
      { name: 'Pen', quantity: 10 },
      { name: 'Pencil', quantity: 0 },
      { name: 'Notebook', quantity: 5 },
      { name: 'Eraser', quantity: 0 }
    ];

    for (const product of inventory) {
      if (product.quantity === 0) {
        console.log(product.name);
      }
    }
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(console.history().map(entry => entry.text).join(',')).should('equal', 'Pencil,Eraser').run('The loop should log only `Pencil` and `Eraser`, the two out-of-stock items', 'Logged the out-of-stock items');
    wrap(source.includes(' of ')).should('equal', true).run('Use a `for...of` loop over `inventory`', 'Used a for...of loop');
    ```

